WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.218
Here is a money value written by hand.

2
00:00:05.218 --> 00:00:07.471
It lists its fields in six places.

3
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:10.348
Someone adds a currency.

4
00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:11.777
Three places get it.

5
00:00:11.777 --> 00:00:14.349
equals, hashCode and toString don't.

6
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:17.932
So is this euro amount equal to this dollar amount?

7
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:19.851
True.

8
00:00:19.851 --> 00:00:25.615
equals still compares only the amount, and the log doesn't even show the currency.

9
00:00:26.433 --> 00:00:27.826
A record says it once.

10
00:00:27.826 --> 00:00:33.145
You list its components in the header, and javac writes the rest from that one list.

11
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:40.529
A private final field and an accessor for each, a canonical constructor, and equals, hashCode and toString.

12
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:44.146
Those last three aren't even written out.

13
00:00:44.146 --> 00:00:52.053
javac hands the JDK the names, minor;currency, and the JDK builds each method on its first run.

14
00:00:52.967 --> 00:00:55.690
Where do you check that an amount isn't negative?

15
00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:59.359
Put the check in a static factory, and new walks straight past it.

16
00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:02.906
A record has one door: the canonical constructor.

17
00:01:02.906 --> 00:01:09.244
Other constructors must call it first, and so do factories, withers and Java deserialization.

18
00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:16.085
Put the check there, in the compact constructor, and it holds for every instance.

19
00:01:16.085 --> 00:01:19.272
It can fix values too: eur becomes EUR.

20
00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.421
A record is only shallowly immutable.

21
00:01:22.421 --> 00:01:25.833
Its fields are final, but what they point to can change.

22
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:28.981
A Batch holds the caller's list of payment ids.

23
00:01:28.981 --> 00:01:31.679
A HashSet files the batch by its hash code.

24
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:34.317
Now the caller adds pay_3 to its list.

25
00:01:34.317 --> 00:01:36.509
Does the set still find the batch?

26
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:38.502
No.

27
00:01:38.502 --> 00:01:41.468
The hash code reads the list, so it changed.

28
00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:45.445
The set looks in the wrong bucket, and contains says false.

29
00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:50.175
The fix is one line in the compact constructor: List.copyOf.

30
00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:53.732
Now the record holds its own unmodifiable copy.

31
00:01:54.633 --> 00:02:01.808
In Kotlin, a data class gets the same methods, but its init block can only reject a value, not rewrite it.

32
00:02:02.633 --> 00:02:07.072
The rule: a record is its components, and javac writes the rest.

33
00:02:07.072 --> 00:02:10.194
The canonical constructor is the only way in.

34
00:02:10.194 --> 00:02:12.829
And immutability stops one level down.

35
00:02:13.033 --> 00:02:13.815
Quick check.

36
00:02:13.815 --> 00:02:18.177
Someone edits a serialized Money and sets the amount to minus five.

37
00:02:18.177 --> 00:02:20.456
What happens when you read it back?

38
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:23.396
It's rejected.

39
00:02:23.396 --> 00:02:28.484
Deserialization calls the canonical constructor too, so your check throws.
